-- Q1: easy / L1 — Net OPEB liability as reconciling item
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$What is the reconciling treatment of the net OPEB liability when converting from governmental fund balance to government-wide net position?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It is subtracted because it is a long-term liability not reported in governmental funds", "It is added because it represents a deferred outflow of resources", "It is ignored because OPEB is reported only in proprietary funds", "It is netted against the pension liability before any adjustment"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): GASB Statement No. 75 requires governments to report the net OPEB liability on government-wide statements. Because governmental funds use a current financial resources measurement focus, they do not report long-term liabilities. The net OPEB liability must be subtracted when reconciling fund balance to net position. Wrong (B): Deferred outflows related to OPEB are a separate adjustment; the liability itself reduces net position. Wrong (C): OPEB liabilities for governmental activities are reported on government-wide statements, not limited to proprietary funds. Wrong (D): Pension and OPEB liabilities are separate line items and are not netted together.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Claims and judgments long-term portion
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$The County of Brookfield reports a $1,800,000 liability for claims and judgments. Of this amount, $400,000 is expected to be paid with current financial resources, and $1,400,000 is long-term. How does the $1,400,000 long-term portion affect the reconciliation from fund balance to net position?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It is added to fund balance because it represents a future resource", "It is subtracted from fund balance because it is a long-term liability excluded from governmental funds", "It is reported as a deferred inflow in the fund statements", "It has no effect because claims and judgments are fully reported at the fund level"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): GASB 34 requires that long-term liabilities not reported in governmental funds be subtracted in the balance sheet reconciliation. Governmental funds only report the current portion of claims and judgments that will be liquidated with current financial resources. The $1,400,000 long-term portion is subtracted to arrive at net position. Wrong (A): Long-term liabilities reduce net position; they are not added. Wrong (C): Claims and judgments are liabilities, not deferred inflows of resources. Wrong (D): Only the current portion ($400,000) appears in fund financial statements; the long-term portion is excluded.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: hard / L3 — Infrastructure assets modified approach
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$The City of Pemberton uses the modified approach under GASB 34 for its road network, which has a historical cost of $85,000,000. The city spent $3,200,000 on road preservation during the year. Analyze how these infrastructure assets and preservation expenditures are treated in the reconciliation from the change in fund balances to the change in net position.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The $3,200,000 is added back as a capital outlay and depreciation of $85,000,000 is subtracted", "The $85,000,000 is added at historical cost and the $3,200,000 is subtracted as depreciation", "The $3,200,000 preservation expenditure remains an expense on both statements because the modified approach reports preservation costs in lieu of depreciation", "The $3,200,000 is capitalized and added to the $85,000,000 historical cost on the government-wide statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the GASB 34 modified approach, eligible infrastructure assets are not depreciated. Instead, preservation and maintenance costs are expensed on the government-wide statements, just as they are expenditures in the fund statements. Therefore, the $3,200,000 preservation expenditure requires no reconciling adjustment for the activity statement — it is an expense under both measurement focuses. Wrong (A): No depreciation is recorded under the modified approach, so there is nothing to subtract. Wrong (B): The modified approach does not depreciate infrastructure assets; applying depreciation contradicts the approach. Wrong (D): Preservation costs are expensed, not capitalized, under the modified approach.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Three categories of net position
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$What are the three categories of net position reported on the government-wide statement of net position?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Nonspendable, restricted, and unrestricted", "Reserved, designated, and undesignated", "Assets, liabilities, and equity", "Net investment in capital assets, restricted, and unrestricted"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): GASB 34, paragraph 32, requires net position to be displayed in three categories: net investment in capital assets, restricted, and unrestricted. These differ from the five fund balance categories used in governmental fund financial statements. Wrong (A): Nonspendable, restricted, committed, assigned, and unassigned are the five fund balance categories under GASB 54, not the net position categories. Wrong (B): Reserved and designated were pre-GASB 54 fund balance classifications that are no longer used. Wrong (C): Assets, liabilities, and equity describe the balance sheet equation, not net position categories.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Construction in progress effect
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$The Town of Fairview spent $6,500,000 on a new municipal building that is still under construction at year-end. The governmental fund reported the full amount as a capital outlay expenditure. Analyze the reconciling adjustment for this construction in progress when converting from the change in fund balances to the change in net position.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The $6,500,000 is added back to the change in net position because it is capitalized as construction in progress on the government-wide statements but is not yet depreciated", "The $6,500,000 remains an expenditure on both statements because the building is incomplete", "The $6,500,000 is partially added back and partially depreciated based on the percentage of completion", "The $6,500,000 is reclassified as a deferred outflow until the building is placed in service"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GASB 34, capital outlay expenditures are added back in the activity statement reconciliation because government-wide statements capitalize these costs. Construction in progress is a capital asset on the statement of net position even though the building is not yet complete. No depreciation is recorded until the asset is placed in service. Wrong (B): The fund expenditure is reversed because government-wide statements capitalize the cost; it does not remain an expenditure. Wrong (C): Percentage of completion is not used for governmental capital asset reconciliation; the full amount is capitalized. Wrong (D): Construction in progress is a capital asset, not a deferred outflow of resources.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Comprehensive multi-adjustment calculation with OPEB and leases
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$The City of Lakewood reports the following data at year-end: governmental fund balances of $8,200,000; capital assets net of depreciation of $31,400,000; general obligation bonds of $16,000,000; compensated absences of $720,000; net pension liability of $4,100,000; net OPEB liability of $2,300,000; GASB 87 lease liability of $950,000; deferred outflows (pensions) of $1,100,000; deferred inflows (pensions) of $600,000; deferred outflows (OPEB) of $380,000; deferred inflows (OPEB) of $210,000; unavailable property tax revenue of $460,000; and internal service fund net position of $340,000. Calculate the net position of governmental activities.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$15,800,000", "$17,000,000", "$21,280,000", "$13,500,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per GASB 34, net position = fund balances ($8,200,000) + capital assets net ($31,400,000) − GO bonds ($16,000,000) − compensated absences ($720,000) − net pension liability ($4,100,000) − net OPEB liability ($2,300,000) − lease liability ($950,000) + deferred outflows pensions ($1,100,000) − deferred inflows pensions ($600,000) + deferred outflows OPEB ($380,000) − deferred inflows OPEB ($210,000) + unavailable revenue ($460,000) + ISF net position ($340,000) = $17,000,000. Wrong (A): This omits the deferred outflows and unavailable revenue adjustments. Wrong (C): This incorrectly adds the long-term liabilities instead of subtracting them. Wrong (D): This double-subtracts the pension-related deferrals.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Encumbrances elimination
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$The City of Grandville reports $750,000 in outstanding encumbrances for purchase orders at year-end. The committed fund balance includes this amount. When preparing the reconciliation from governmental fund balance to government-wide net position, how are these encumbrances treated?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["They are added to net position as a capital asset", "They are subtracted as a long-term liability", "They require no adjustment because encumbrances are a fund-level budgetary mechanism that does not appear on government-wide statements, and the committed fund balance carries through without reclassification", "They are reclassified as a deferred outflow of resources"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB 34, encumbrances are budgetary accounts used at the fund level. They do not create assets, liabilities, or expenses on the government-wide statements. The committed fund balance that reserves resources for encumbrances flows into the reconciliation as part of total fund balance without a separate adjusting entry. Wrong (A): Encumbrances represent commitments, not completed transactions — no capital asset exists yet. Wrong (B): Outstanding purchase orders are not long-term liabilities; the goods or services have not been received. Wrong (D): Encumbrances are not deferred outflows; they are budgetary reservations with no accrual-basis equivalent.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L2 — Prepaids consumption vs purchases method
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$A county reports $180,000 in prepaid insurance on its governmental fund balance sheet using the consumption method. On the government-wide statement of net position, how is this prepaid asset reported?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It is eliminated because government-wide statements do not report prepaid items", "It is reclassified as a deferred outflow of resources", "It is reduced by the amount of the current year expense", "It is reported at the same $180,000 amount with no reconciling adjustment needed"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): When a governmental fund uses the consumption method, prepaid items are already reported as assets on the fund balance sheet. Government-wide statements also report prepaids as assets under full accrual. Since both statements recognize the same asset at the same amount, no reconciling adjustment is required. Wrong (A): Government-wide statements do report prepaid items as current assets. Wrong (B): Prepaid insurance is an asset, not a deferred outflow of resources under GASB 63. Wrong (C): Both statements reflect the same year-end balance; no additional expense reduction is needed in the reconciliation.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Fiduciary funds exclusion
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$Which fund type is excluded from the government-wide financial statements and therefore does not require a reconciliation to governmental activities?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Fiduciary funds", "Special revenue funds", "Internal service funds", "Debt service funds"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): GASB 34, paragraph 12, excludes fiduciary funds from government-wide financial statements because the resources in fiduciary funds are not available to finance the government's own programs. Therefore, no reconciliation between fiduciary fund statements and government-wide statements is required. Wrong (B): Special revenue funds are governmental funds that are included in the reconciliation to governmental activities. Wrong (C): Internal service funds are included with governmental activities on the government-wide statements and require reconciliation. Wrong (D): Debt service funds are governmental funds included in the fund-to-government-wide reconciliation.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — GASB 87 lease liability reconciliation
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$The Town of Millbrook entered a 10-year lease for office space with annual payments of $250,000. The governmental fund reports each payment as an expenditure. The government-wide statements report a right-of-use asset of $1,850,000 (net of amortization) and a lease liability of $1,920,000. Analyze the balance sheet reconciliation adjustments required for this lease.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Add $1,920,000 for the lease liability and subtract $1,850,000 for the right-of-use asset", "Add $1,850,000 for the right-of-use asset and subtract $1,920,000 for the lease liability, producing a net reduction of $70,000 to fund balance", "Add the right-of-use asset and ignore the lease liability since it is an operating item", "Subtract both the right-of-use asset and the lease liability from fund balance"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 87, government-wide statements report both the right-of-use asset (added, +$1,850,000) and the lease liability (subtracted, −$1,920,000). Neither appears on the governmental fund balance sheet. The net effect is a $70,000 reduction when reconciling fund balance to net position. This reflects accumulated amortization exceeding principal payments to date. Wrong (A): This reverses the direction — the asset is added and the liability is subtracted, not the other way around. Wrong (C): GASB 87 lease liabilities must be reported on government-wide statements; they cannot be ignored. Wrong (D): The right-of-use asset increases net position; it is added, not subtracted.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Gain on early extinguishment of debt
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$The City of Edgemont retired $4,000,000 in bonds early by paying $3,750,000 from its debt service fund. The bonds had an unamortized premium of $80,000. Analyze the reconciling adjustments needed on the activity statement reconciliation for this early extinguishment.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Remove the $3,750,000 expenditure and recognize a $250,000 gain", "Add back the $3,750,000 debt service expenditure and recognize no gain or loss", "Remove the $3,750,000 expenditure, add back the $4,000,000 principal reduction and the $80,000 premium, and recognize a $330,000 gain on early extinguishment", "Remove the $3,750,000 expenditure and recognize a $170,000 loss"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): The fund recorded a $3,750,000 expenditure. Government-wide removes this expenditure (+$3,750,000), reduces bonds payable (−$4,000,000) and the unamortized premium (−$80,000), resulting in removal of a total carrying amount of $4,080,000. The gain equals the carrying amount ($4,080,000) minus the cash paid ($3,750,000) = $330,000. Wrong (A): This ignores the unamortized premium, which increases the carrying amount and the resulting gain. Wrong (B): When the carrying amount exceeds the cash paid, a gain must be recognized on the government-wide statements. Wrong (D): The government paid less than the carrying amount, so there is a gain, not a loss.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Donated capital assets at fair value
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$A county receives a donated parcel of land with a fair value of $2,400,000. No cash changes hands. The governmental funds report no transaction. On the government-wide statements, how is this donation reported and what reconciliation adjustment is required?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No adjustment is needed because no cash was exchanged", "The land is reported at historical cost to the donor on the government-wide statements", "The land is reported as a deferred inflow in both the fund and government-wide statements", "The land is capitalized at $2,400,000 fair value on the government-wide statements and revenue of $2,400,000 is recognized, requiring both a capital asset addition and a revenue adjustment in the reconciliation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): GASB 34 requires donated capital assets to be reported at acquisition value (fair value at time of donation) on the government-wide statements. The donation is also recognized as revenue. Since governmental funds record neither the asset nor the revenue (no current financial resources are involved), both adjustments appear in the reconciliation. Wrong (A): The absence of cash does not eliminate the need for government-wide recognition; donated assets are reported at fair value. Wrong (B): GASB standards require acquisition value (fair value), not the donor's historical cost. Wrong (C): Donated capital assets are recognized as revenue, not deferred inflows, on the government-wide statements.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Accumulated depreciation on balance sheet
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$What role does accumulated depreciation play in the balance sheet reconciliation from governmental fund balance to net position?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Capital assets are added at their net amount (cost minus accumulated depreciation), reducing the positive adjustment compared to gross cost", "Accumulated depreciation is reported as a separate liability on the government-wide statements", "Accumulated depreciation is added to fund balance as a contra-asset", "Accumulated depreciation has no effect because it is only reported in the notes to the financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): GASB 34 requires the balance sheet reconciliation to add capital assets used in governmental activities net of accumulated depreciation. The accumulated depreciation reduces the gross cost of capital assets, resulting in a smaller positive adjustment to convert fund balance to net position. Wrong (B): Accumulated depreciation is a contra-asset, not a liability. Wrong (C): Accumulated depreciation reduces the capital asset balance; it is not independently added to fund balance. Wrong (D): Accumulated depreciation is reported on the face of the government-wide statement of net position, not only in the notes.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Enterprise fund vs ISF in reconciliation
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$Which proprietary fund type is consolidated with governmental activities in the government-wide reconciliation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Enterprise funds", "Internal service funds", "Special revenue funds", "Pension trust funds"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): GASB 34 requires internal service fund balances and activities to be consolidated with governmental activities on the government-wide statements because their primary customers are governmental funds. This means the reconciliation must add internal service fund net position and changes in net position to governmental activities. Wrong (A): Enterprise funds are reported as business-type activities on the government-wide statements, not consolidated with governmental activities. Wrong (C): Special revenue funds are governmental funds already included in the fund financial statements being reconciled. Wrong (D): Pension trust funds are fiduciary funds excluded from government-wide statements entirely.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Capital asset impairment
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$The City of Rockdale determines that a government building suffered a permanent impairment of $1,200,000 due to environmental contamination. The impairment is recognized on the government-wide statements. How does this impairment affect the activity statement reconciliation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The impairment is added back because it is a capital-related adjustment", "The impairment is reported as an expenditure in both fund and government-wide statements", "The impairment loss of $1,200,000 is subtracted in the reconciliation because it is an expense on the government-wide statements but not an expenditure in the governmental funds", "The impairment is netted against depreciation expense with no separate disclosure"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB 42, capital asset impairments are recognized as losses or expenses on the government-wide statements. Because governmental funds do not report capital assets, no expenditure is recorded at the fund level. The $1,200,000 impairment must be subtracted in the reconciliation from the change in fund balances to the change in net position. Wrong (A): Impairment reduces net position; it is subtracted, not added back. Wrong (B): Governmental funds do not record impairment losses because they do not report capital assets. Wrong (D): GASB 42 impairments are disclosed separately from regular depreciation expense.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Landfill closure and postclosure costs
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$A county operates a municipal landfill accounted for in the general fund. During the year, the estimated landfill closure and postclosure liability increased by $850,000 under GASB 18. The county made no cash payments toward closure costs. How does this $850,000 appear in the activity statement reconciliation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It is added back to the change in fund balances", "It is reported as a deferred inflow on both statements", "It requires no reconciliation because landfill costs are reported identically on both statements", "It is subtracted from the change in fund balances because the increase in the long-term liability is an expense on government-wide statements but not a fund expenditure"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB 18, landfill closure and postclosure costs are recognized as a long-term liability on government-wide statements as capacity is consumed. Governmental funds only record expenditures when payments are due. Since no cash payment was made, the $850,000 increase in the liability is an expense only on government-wide statements and must be subtracted in the reconciliation. Wrong (A): Adding the amount back would be incorrect; the liability increase reduces net position. Wrong (B): Landfill closure costs create a liability, not a deferred inflow of resources. Wrong (C): The treatments differ because governmental funds use modified accrual and do not recognize long-term liabilities.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: easy / L2 — Prepaids purchases method reconciliation
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$A government uses the purchases method for supplies in its general fund. At year-end, $95,000 of supplies remain on hand. The fund reports no asset for supplies inventory. How does this affect the balance sheet reconciliation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The $95,000 must be added when reconciling to net position because government-wide statements report the supplies inventory as an asset under full accrual", "The $95,000 is subtracted from fund balance", "No adjustment is needed because the purchases method is also used government-wide", "The $95,000 is reported as a deferred outflow of resources"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the purchases method, governmental funds expense supplies when purchased and report no ending inventory. Government-wide statements use full accrual accounting and report the $95,000 of supplies on hand as a current asset. The reconciliation must add this asset to convert fund balance to net position. Wrong (B): The supplies represent an asset that increases net position; they are added, not subtracted. Wrong (C): Government-wide statements always use full accrual, regardless of the method the fund uses; an adjustment is required. Wrong (D): Supplies inventory is a current asset, not a deferred outflow of resources under GASB 63.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q18: hard / L4 — Multi-adjustment activity statement with pension deferrals
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$The City of Harborview reports a net change in governmental fund balances of $1,600,000. The following information is available: capital outlay expenditures of $4,200,000; depreciation expense of $2,100,000; bond principal payments of $1,500,000; new bond proceeds of $3,000,000; increase in accrued interest payable of $45,000; pension contributions (fund expenditures) of $900,000; pension expense (government-wide) of $1,150,000; increase in compensated absences liability of $180,000; internal service fund change in net position of $95,000; and decrease in unavailable revenue (deferred inflows) of $120,000. Calculate the change in net position of governmental activities.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$2,300,000", "$1,700,000", "$1,900,000", "$1,100,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Start with fund change $1,600,000. Add capital outlay +$4,200,000. Subtract depreciation −$2,100,000. Add principal payments +$1,500,000. Subtract bond proceeds −$3,000,000. Subtract accrued interest increase −$45,000. Pension adjustment: contributions ($900,000) added back, pension expense ($1,150,000) subtracted, net −$250,000. Subtract compensated absences increase −$180,000. Add ISF change +$95,000. Subtract unavailable revenue decrease −$120,000. Total = $1,700,000. Wrong (A): This incorrectly adds the unavailable revenue decrease instead of subtracting it. Wrong (C): This omits the pension and compensated absences adjustments. Wrong (D): This double-counts the bond proceeds deduction.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Net investment in capital assets calculation
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$The City of Cedarville reports capital assets of $48,000,000 with accumulated depreciation of $16,500,000. Outstanding capital-related debt includes general obligation bonds of $18,000,000 (of which $2,500,000 is unspent bond proceeds held in a capital projects fund) and a GASB 87 lease liability of $1,400,000. Deferred amounts on refundings total $600,000 (deferred outflow). Calculate the net investment in capital assets component of net position.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$31,500,000", "$12,100,000", "$15,200,000", "$13,700,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per GASB 34, paragraph 33, net investment in capital assets = capital assets net of depreciation ($48,000,000 − $16,500,000 = $31,500,000) minus capital-related debt ($18,000,000 + $1,400,000 = $19,400,000) plus unspent bond proceeds ($2,500,000) plus deferred amount on refunding ($600,000) = $31,500,000 − $19,400,000 + $2,500,000 + $600,000 = $15,200,000. Wrong (A): This is simply capital assets net of depreciation without adjusting for related debt. Wrong (B): This omits the unspent bond proceeds and deferred refunding amount. Wrong (D): This incorrectly subtracts the deferred amount on refunding instead of adding it.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — Special assessments debt reconciliation
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$A government issues $6,000,000 in special assessment bonds for a sidewalk improvement project. The government is obligated in some manner for the debt. The special revenue fund records the bond proceeds as other financing sources and the construction costs as expenditures. Evaluate the reconciling treatment of these transactions on both the balance sheet and activity statement reconciliations, considering that the government is obligated for the debt.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No adjustment is needed because special assessment bonds are off-balance-sheet financing", "Both the asset and liability are reported identically in fund and government-wide statements", "The debt is excluded from government-wide statements because property owners are primarily liable", "The $6,000,000 debt is reported as a long-term liability on the government-wide statements, requiring subtraction in the balance sheet reconciliation, and the bond proceeds are removed as revenue on the activity reconciliation while the constructed asset is capitalized"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB 6, when a government is obligated in some manner for special assessment debt, the debt is reported as a general long-term liability on the government-wide statements. The balance sheet reconciliation subtracts the debt and adds the capitalized infrastructure asset. The activity reconciliation removes the bond proceeds (not revenue government-wide) and adds back the capital outlay expenditure (capitalized government-wide). Wrong (A): If the government is obligated in some manner, the debt must appear on government-wide statements. Wrong (B): Governmental funds do not report long-term debt or capital assets, so the presentations differ. Wrong (C): GASB 6 requires reporting the debt when the government has any obligation, regardless of who is primarily liable.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Pollution remediation obligations
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$The City of Greenfield recognizes a $3,400,000 pollution remediation obligation under GASB 49 on its government-wide statements. The governmental funds report only $200,000 in current-year remediation expenditures paid in cash. Analyze the net reconciling adjustment on the activity statement for pollution remediation.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The $3,200,000 difference is subtracted in the reconciliation because the government-wide statements recognize the full estimated liability as an expense, while the fund statements only record cash payments as expenditures", "The $3,400,000 is added to the change in fund balances", "The $200,000 cash payment is reversed because it is not an expense government-wide", "No adjustment is needed because pollution costs are expensed identically under both methods"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): GASB 49 requires the full estimated remediation obligation to be recognized on government-wide statements when obligating events occur. Governmental funds only recognize expenditures when current financial resources are used ($200,000). The difference of $3,200,000 ($3,400,000 liability recognized minus $200,000 paid) must be subtracted in the reconciliation because it is an additional expense on government-wide statements. Wrong (B): The liability increase reduces net position; it is subtracted, not added. Wrong (C): The $200,000 cash payment is a valid expense on both statements and should not be reversed. Wrong (D): Modified accrual only reports cash payments; full accrual recognizes the entire obligation, creating a difference.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Restricted net position vs restricted fund balance
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$A government reports restricted fund balance of $2,800,000 in its governmental funds for debt service. When converting to government-wide net position, the restricted category shows only $1,500,000 for debt service. Analyze why restricted net position differs from restricted fund balance for the same purpose.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Restricted fund balance and restricted net position always equal each other", "Restricted net position is lower because it reflects the net effect of restricted assets minus related liabilities, while restricted fund balance reflects only the current financial resources set aside; the difference arises because debt service fund assets must be offset against accrued interest payable and other related obligations on the government-wide statements", "Restricted net position is always higher because it includes capital assets", "The difference is caused by rounding and reporting presentation choices"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 34, restricted net position represents the net amount of restricted assets minus related liabilities. The restricted fund balance only reflects current financial resources (cash, receivables) designated for debt service. Government-wide statements offset restricted assets against related liabilities such as accrued interest payable and matured debt, which reduces the restricted net position below the restricted fund balance. Wrong (A): The two measures use different measurement focuses and rarely equal each other. Wrong (C): Capital assets are not typically included in debt-service-related restricted net position; they fall under net investment in capital assets. Wrong (D): The difference is systematic, arising from the different measurement focuses, not from rounding.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Change in compensated absences liability calculation
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$The County of Madison reports compensated absences liability of $1,100,000 at the beginning of the year and $1,350,000 at year-end on the government-wide statements. During the year, the governmental funds paid $420,000 in compensated absences from current resources. Analyze the reconciling adjustment on the activity statement.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Add $250,000 to the change in fund balances", "Subtract $420,000 from the change in fund balances", "Subtract $250,000 from the change in fund balances because the increase in the long-term liability represents an additional expense on government-wide statements beyond the cash paid in the funds", "Add $420,000 and subtract $670,000 from the change in fund balances"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): The net increase in compensated absences is $1,350,000 − $1,100,000 = $250,000. Governmental funds expensed $420,000 in cash payments. Government-wide statements recognized $420,000 + $250,000 = $670,000 in total compensated absences expense. The reconciling adjustment subtracts $250,000 (the increase in the long-term liability not recognized in fund expenditures). Wrong (A): The increase in a long-term liability reduces net position; it is subtracted. Wrong (B): The $420,000 was already recorded as an expenditure in the funds and as an expense government-wide; it requires no adjustment. Wrong (D): This double-counts the adjustment; only the net change in the liability ($250,000) needs to be subtracted.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Amortization of deferred amount on refunding
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$The City of Westfield has a deferred amount on refunding of $1,800,000 from a prior-year advance refunding. During the current year, $180,000 was amortized. Governmental funds recorded no transaction for this amortization. Analyze the reconciling adjustment on the activity statement.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Add $180,000 because the amortization creates revenue government-wide", "Subtract $1,800,000 for the full deferred amount", "Add $1,800,000 to reflect the original refunding transaction", "Subtract $180,000 from the change in fund balances because the amortization of the deferred amount increases interest expense on the government-wide statements but is not recorded in the funds"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): GASB 65, paragraph 6, requires the deferred amount on refunding to be amortized over the shorter of the life of the old or new debt. This amortization increases interest expense on government-wide statements. Since governmental funds do not record the amortization (no current financial resources are used), the $180,000 must be subtracted in the activity statement reconciliation. Wrong (A): Amortization of a deferred amount on refunding is an expense, not revenue. Wrong (B): Only the current-year amortization ($180,000) affects the activity statement reconciliation, not the entire balance. Wrong (C): The original refunding was recorded in a prior year; it does not affect the current activity statement reconciliation.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Tax revenue timing difference (earned vs available)
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$A school district levies $12,000,000 in property taxes for the current fiscal year. By year-end, $11,200,000 has been collected. Of the $800,000 remaining, $500,000 is expected within 60 days and $300,000 is expected between 61 and 120 days. The district uses a 60-day availability period. What amount of property tax revenue does the district report on its government-wide statement of activities?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$12,000,000 because government-wide statements recognize revenue when the enforceable legal claim arises, regardless of collection timing", "$11,200,000 because only cash collections are recognized", "$11,700,000 because only amounts collected within 60 days are included", "$11,500,000 because a 10% allowance for doubtful accounts must be deducted"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): GASB 33 and GASB 34 require property tax revenue on government-wide statements to be recognized on the full accrual basis — when the enforceable legal claim arises (the levy date). The full $12,000,000 is revenue government-wide. The $300,000 beyond the 60-day window is a deferred inflow only on fund statements, creating a reconciling adjustment. Wrong (B): Government-wide statements use full accrual, not cash basis. Wrong (C): The 60-day availability period applies only to governmental fund (modified accrual) statements. Wrong (D): There is no indication of uncollectible taxes; the full levy is recognized.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: easy / L2 — Bond discount amortization effect
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$A government issued bonds at a discount. On the government-wide statements, the bond discount is amortized over the life of the bonds. How does this amortization affect interest expense on the government-wide statements compared to the fund statements?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It decreases interest expense on government-wide statements", "It increases interest expense on government-wide statements because the amortization of the discount adds to the coupon interest each period", "It has no effect on interest expense", "It converts interest expense into a capital outlay"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Bond discount amortization increases interest expense on government-wide statements. The discount represents additional borrowing cost spread over the bond's life. Each period, the amortization is added to the coupon interest payment to determine total interest expense. Governmental funds do not amortize the discount; they recorded it as a reduction of other financing sources at issuance. Wrong (A): Premium amortization decreases interest expense; discount amortization increases it. Wrong (C): Discount amortization directly affects interest expense each period on the government-wide statements. Wrong (D): Discount amortization is a component of interest expense, not a capital outlay.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Interfund eliminations in government-wide statements
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$The City of Glenwood has interfund payables of $600,000 from the general fund to the capital projects fund. Both are governmental funds. Analyze the treatment of these interfund balances in the reconciliation to the government-wide statement of net position.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The interfund payable and receivable are both added to net position", "The interfund balances are reclassified as external liabilities on the government-wide statements", "The interfund payable and receivable are eliminated in the government-wide statements because both funds are within governmental activities, resulting in no net effect on the reconciliation", "The interfund balances are converted to deferred inflows and deferred outflows"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): GASB 34, paragraph 58, requires interfund receivables and payables between funds within the same activity type (governmental activities) to be eliminated on the government-wide statements. Since the general fund payable equals the capital projects fund receivable, they net to zero. This elimination has no net effect on the reconciliation because the assets and liabilities offset. Wrong (A): Interfund balances within the same activity are eliminated, not added. Wrong (B): These are internal balances between governmental funds, not external liabilities to outside parties. Wrong (D): Interfund balances are eliminated, not reclassified as deferred inflows or outflows.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Unrestricted net position definition
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$What does the unrestricted component of net position on the government-wide statements represent?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Only cash and cash equivalents available for any purpose", "Resources restricted by external creditors or grantors", "Capital assets minus accumulated depreciation", "The residual amount of net position after subtracting net investment in capital assets and restricted net position"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): GASB 34, paragraph 35, defines unrestricted net position as the residual amount remaining after net investment in capital assets and restricted net position are determined. It may be positive or negative and is not limited to cash. Wrong (A): Unrestricted net position includes all types of unrestricted assets minus unrestricted liabilities, not just cash. Wrong (B): Resources restricted by external parties are classified as restricted net position, not unrestricted. Wrong (C): Capital assets minus depreciation is the starting point for net investment in capital assets, a separate component.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: hard / L4 — Comprehensive balance sheet reconciliation with GASB 87 and pensions
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$The Town of Silverton reports: governmental fund balances of $5,600,000; capital assets (net) of $19,800,000; general obligation bonds of $11,200,000; unamortized bond premium of $350,000; GASB 87 right-of-use assets (net) of $2,100,000; GASB 87 lease liabilities of $2,250,000; compensated absences of $480,000; net pension liability of $3,800,000; deferred outflows (pensions) of $920,000; deferred inflows (pensions) of $540,000; net OPEB liability of $1,600,000; deferred outflows (OPEB) of $310,000; deferred inflows (OPEB) of $190,000; unavailable grant revenue of $275,000; and internal service fund net position of $205,000. Calculate the net position of governmental activities.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$10,400,000", "$9,100,000", "$7,250,000", "$8,800,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Net position = $5,600,000 + $19,800,000 + $2,100,000 − $11,200,000 − $350,000 − $2,250,000 − $480,000 − $3,800,000 + $920,000 − $540,000 − $1,600,000 + $310,000 − $190,000 + $275,000 + $205,000 = $10,400,000. Each adjustment follows GASB 34: add capital assets and ROU assets, subtract all long-term liabilities (bonds, premium, leases, compensated absences, pension, OPEB), add/subtract pension and OPEB deferrals, add unavailable revenue, and add ISF net position. Wrong (B): This omits the bond premium and lease liability deductions. Wrong (C): This incorrectly subtracts the deferred outflows instead of adding them. Wrong (D): This double-subtracts the OPEB-related deferrals.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Capital asset threshold and capitalization policy
(4, 'Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation', $EXPL$A county's capitalization policy sets a $10,000 threshold for capital assets. The general fund purchased 50 office chairs at $400 each ($20,000 total) and a server for $45,000. In the reconciliation from the change in fund balances to the change in net position, what is the capital asset adjustment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Add back $65,000 for both purchases", "Add back $45,000 for the server only because the individual chairs fall below the $10,000 capitalization threshold and remain an expense on both statements", "Add back $20,000 for the chairs only", "No adjustment is needed because both items are below the threshold"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Government capitalization policies set minimum thresholds for individual items. Each chair costs $400, which is below the $10,000 threshold, so the chairs are expensed on both fund and government-wide statements. The $45,000 server exceeds the threshold and is capitalized government-wide, requiring a $45,000 add-back in the reconciliation. Wrong (A): The chairs are not capitalized because each individual item is below the capitalization threshold; aggregate cost does not override per-item thresholds. Wrong (C): The chairs are below threshold and are not capitalized. Wrong (D): The server at $45,000 clearly exceeds the $10,000 threshold and must be capitalized.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
